2026-05-12  Nail #8: grand-map resources + tile→state causation
  WHAT: Resources now live on the world grand map as
        scattered patches (BFS-grown blobs of grass tiles)
        with small icons rendered at each patch centroid.
        Currently shipping FOREST (green W) and ORE (gray O);
        the table is extensible — adding a new resource is a
        single ResourceDef entry in resources.ts. State-
        level resources (the icon you see when you zoom in)
        are now DERIVED from tile resources within the
        state's footprint: plurality wins. If a state has
        no scattered resource at all, it falls back to
        FARMLAND or TRADE via rng. Net effect: if 3/4 of
        a region's tiles are mountains, ~3/4 of its states
        come out as ORE — the user's "grand map shows 3/4
        mountains → 3/4 mining states" causation.
  WHY:  Exact user words: "I'd want to see at the zoomed
        out scale of an area has, ore, forests, or other
        resource I will add later on. Write the code in
        such a way that on the grand map we see these
        scattered through randomly with the option to add
        more by adding a new data type. The grand scale
        resources on the map should tie to the zoomed in
        territory production boosts." Now the strategic
        and territory views are causally linked: where you
        SEE ore = where you'll MINE ore (when the mining
        line lands).

  Architecture:
   - resources.ts (NEW): RESOURCE_DEFS table — single source
     of truth. Each entry: id (= array index), key, label,
     color, letter, scatterOnMap, patchCount, patchSize,
     matchingLine. Adding a new resource = append one entry
     (and adjust matchingLine when the building line lands).
   - world.ts: Region.tileCount unchanged; added World.
     resourceOf (Int16Array, parallel to regionOf, -1 if no
     resource) and World.patches[] (centroid list, one per
     scattered blob). Procgen step 9 walks scatteredResource
     Defs() and BFS-grows patchCount blobs of patchSize tiles
     each per resource. Step 10 derives each state.resource
     from tile-resource plurality within that state's tiles;
     fallback to fallbackResource(rng) (currently FARMLAND
     / TRADE) for no-dominant-resource states.
   - buildings.ts: decoupled from world.ts. Now just owns
     line names + tier data; no longer imports resource
     constants. matchingLine moved to resources.ts as
     matchingLineFor(resourceId).
   - mapResourceLayer.ts (NEW): renders one icon per patch
     at patch centroid. Uses getResourceDef for color +
     letter — picks up new entries automatically.
   - stateLayer.ts / stateMenu.ts: switched to
     getResourceDef + matchingLineFor. stateMenu handles
     null matchingLine ("no matching line yet (mining
     line pending)") so ORE states display sensibly.
   - main.ts: instantiates mapResourceLayer in loadMap
     above terrain/tint/border (z-order: above map base,
     below ai-battle markers and armies).

  Notes for future:
   - Patch count + size are tunables in RESOURCE_DEFS.
     Bump or trim per resource for density tuning.
   - Resource placement is currently UNBIASED (random
     grass tile). Future polish: bias by terrain
     (ore in inland / high-elevation, fish on coast,
     etc.). Hook is just an extra predicate in the seed-
     pick loop of procgen step 9.
   - Mining line still pending — when added, set ORE's
     matchingLine: 'mining' in RESOURCE_DEFS and the menu
     ★ + halo light up automatically.
   - "3/4 land = mountain → 3/4 mining" causation:
     achievable by bumping ORE's patchCount/patchSize so
     the dominant resource saturates more states. If the
     correlation feels weak, tune those numbers up.

