2026-05-12  Nail #7: territory zoom + states + build menu
  WHAT: Tap a region in strategic view to SNAP-ZOOM into its
        interior — like the strategic/tactical battle scene
        transition, but for territories. Inside the snap:
        each region is sub-partitioned into 3-6 procgen
        "states," every state shows a resource icon
        (farmland / woods / trade hub), and tapping a state
        opens a build menu with three lines (Settlement /
        Forestry / Merchant). Each line has 3 tiers. Build
        is instant and free this nail; matching the line to
        the state's resource lights up a star marker on the
        UI and a white outline on the badge (the
        "production boost" hook lands in the next nail).
        Back button (top-left) exits the snap. Player-vs-AI
        engagement detection is suspended while zoomed in
        so the camera doesn't swap scenes mid-build.
  WHY:  User's idea, exact words: "Let's do the same snap
        we do for tactical/strategic battle mode but for
        each territory. Each territory should (when you
        snap in we see a zoomed in version of that land.
        Divided by let's call them states. Each state has a
        resource icon of what it produces the most and if
        you build that there you get a production boost.
        WOW. Now THAT is a novel idea." It is novel — and
        it gives the strategic map a third zoom-tier
        (strategic / territory / tactical) for free.
        Costs / production / map resources / mining line all
        deferred to follow-up nails per user's scoping
        ("build the buildings, shape the map to have the
        resources, then decide costs"). This nail proves
        out the UI shape end-to-end.

  Architecture:
   - world.ts: Region.stateIds[], World.states[],
     World.stateOf (parallel to regionOf). Procgen step 8
     sub-partitions each region via a per-region Voronoi-
     style multi-source BFS within the region tiles. K
     scales with region size (clamp 3..6). Each state gets
     a resource type (RESOURCE_FARMLAND / WOODS / TRADE,
     uniform random for now). All deterministic from world
     seed.
   - buildings.ts: per-state tier record (settlement /
     forestry / merchant tiers 0..3). initBuildings on
     loadMap; buildNext(stateId, line) bumps tier or
     rejects at max. matchingLine(resource) -> the line
     that pairs 1:1 with that resource.
   - stateLayer.ts: pre-bakes state borders + resource
     icons per region; refreshRegionBuildings(rid) re-draws
     just the building-icon stack for one region.
     setVisibleRegion(rid|null) toggles which region's
     overlay is shown (only one at a time).
   - input.ts: gained an onTap callback. Pointer tracked
     start position; if displacement <= 12px and no drag
     handler hijacked and no second pointer ever joined,
     pointerup fires onTap. Single-finger only.
   - main.ts: RegionZoom state machine (inactive →
     entering → active → exiting → inactive). Mirrors the
     battleTransition lerp. Strategic layer dims to ~0.45
     and the state overlay fades in during entry; reverse
     on exit. preZoomCamera snapshot for the restore path.
     Tap routing: in 'active' mode, tap = state-tap → open
     build menu (in-active region only); otherwise tap =
     region-tap → start zoom.
   - HTML/CSS: bottom-anchored #state-menu modal (mobile
     target: 360×740 — card sits at the bottom with big
     tap targets); top-left #region-zoom-back button
     mirroring the existing top-right new-map / recruit
     buttons.
   - Engagement gate: tickEngagements now skips player-vs-
     AI pairs while regionZoom is anything but inactive,
     so the battle-snap and region-snap never collide.

  Notes for future:
   - Map resources scattered on tiles (user's flagged
     follow-up). Will gate mining line + add the actual
     production effects per resource.
   - AI doesn't build anything yet — only the player can
     enter region-zoom and tap states. AIs progress
     economically through the same hooks once we wire
     building effects.
   - Costs (user explicitly deferred: "I wanna build the
     buildings, shape the map to have the resources, then
     decide costs"). Future-friendly: the buildNext()
     return-bool already has a place to reject a build
     for any reason (cost, prereq, etc).

