2026-05-11  Long-range player movement (pathfinding) [SUPERSEDED]
  Reverted by the entry above — see "Movement model: STRAIGHT
  LINE or reject." Kept for historical record of the iteration.

  WHAT: Player can drag their army to any region on the map,
        not just an immediate graph-neighbor. BFS through
        the region graph at drop time finds a path. Transit
        rule: source + destination always passable;
        intermediate regions must be neutral or player-
        owned. Enemy regions block transit but are OK as the
        destination (so you can land on enemy land — capture
        and combat still apply). No path = drop rejected,
        previous march resumes. Army marches the polyline
        through the path's region centroids.
        AIs still straight-line via marchTo (they don't path-
        find). Future state allows multiple armies per
        nation — the pathfinder is per-call, so it scales
        cleanly to any number of armies.
  WHY:  Single-hop drag made the map feel cramped: to attack
        across the map you'd have to drag-stop-drag-stop
        through every region in between. With graph
        pathfinding, you set a long-range objective once and
        the army gets there along the legal path. The
        ownership rule encodes the meaningful constraint —
        enemy land is impassable as transit, which makes
        controlling borders strategically important.

  Architecture:
   - army.ts: march is now a waypoint queue
     ({ waypoints[], idx, targetRegionId }). tick advances
     through points; redrawMarchLine draws the polyline.
   - army.ts: ArmyDeps gains optional `pathfind(from, to)`
     callback. endDrag delegates routing to it: same region
     drop = single waypoint; different region = pathfind →
     waypoints (centroids of intermediates + drop point at
     final region); no path = reject + return false.
   - main.ts: pathfindForNation(nationId, from, to) — BFS
     with the "owned or neutral, or destination" passability
     predicate. Wired only on player's army create call
     (AIs straight-line via marchTo).
   - marchTo still takes a single (x, y) — wraps to a one-
     waypoint march internally. AI call sites unchanged.

  Notes for future:
   - AI symmetry: AIs straight-line through enemy land. If
     this feels exploitable, wire pathfindForNation into
     marchTo too. (Currently scope-creep beyond the user's
     ask.)
   - Path is committed at drop time. If an enemy captures a
     region the path goes through mid-march, the army keeps
     going. Re-routing on mid-flight ownership changes is a
     future polish nail.
   - The transit predicate (`owner === nationId || owner < 0
     || nb === to`) is the natural hook for a war/alliance
     system: enemies at war become passable; allies become
     passable too.

