2026-05-09  #6.2 follow-up #4: variety + visible simulation
  WHAT: Three things. (1) Both sides now roll randomized
        starting compositions per map seed — total in [40,100]
        soldiers per side with cav share in [10%,50%], so each
        "new map" gives a different matchup. Same seed = same
        rolls, fully deterministic. (2) The action menu shows
        a small panel above the three cards listing each
        side's composition ("YOU: inf X · cav Y" / "ENEMY:
        ..."), so the player can see what they're up against
        before picking Attack/Simulate/Intimidate. (3) The
        Simulate result panel now animates round-by-round:
        each round renders for ~300ms (compressed for long
        battles to stay under a ~3.5s budget), with a round
        counter ("Round 3 / 12") and force bars per side that
        shrink as power drops. DISMISS button appears only
        after the animation completes.
  WHY:  User played the flow and flagged three real issues:
        same units every map (boring), can't actually see the
        simulation (single counter abstracted it away), and
        no info about enemy composition pre-pick (how do you
        decide between Attack and Simulate if you don't know
        the matchup). All three were genuine gaps — adding
        them turns Simulate from "press button, see number"
        into "watch the battle unfold" and gives the menu
        decisions actual weight.

  Implementation: exported makeRng from world.ts so other
        systems can derive deterministic streams from the
        same seed. main.ts gained rollComposition(rng) and
        applyCompositions(seed) helpers, called once at
        module init and once per loadMap after army+enemy are
        created (XOR'd seed so the comp rolls don't share RNG
        state with map generation). battleSim.ts now records
        a SimSnapshot per round (regiments + power for both
        sides) plus a refPower for force-bar normalization
        (max of starting powers, ≥1). battleMenu.ts rewritten:
        show() takes both sides' regiments and renders the
        pre-pick panel, showResult() walks snapshots via
        setTimeout (cancellable), updating round text,
        regiment lines, and bar widths each step. New HTML
        nodes #battle-menu-pre, #battle-result-round, plus
        per-side .bar / .bar-fill elements. CSS uses the
        same hidden-attribute override pattern as before.

